LEFT PAGE. (1) Statement. Let F(z) = integral from 0 to z of dz/sqrt(1 - mu^2 sin^2 z), the incomplete elliptic integral of the first kind in amplitude form with modulus mu. Take a spherical triangle with sides a, b, c and opposite angles A, B, C, and suppose sin A/sin a = sin B/sin b = sin C/sin c = mu, so the law-of-sines constant is the modulus. Gauss asserts F(a) + F(b) + F(c) = F(pi). F(pi) = 2K. He writes 'Fz', but c is clearly meant. A struck-out symbol appears before F(pi).

Justification (mine). By the law of cosines, cos c = cos a cos b + sin a sin b cos C, and cos C = +sqrt(1 - mu^2 sin^2 c) when C is acute. Put sigma = pi - c. Since Delta(sigma) = Delta(c), this becomes cos sigma = cos a cos b - sin a sin b Delta(sigma). That is exactly Lagrange's form of Euler's addition theorem, so F(a) + F(b) = F(sigma) = F(pi - c) = 2K - F(c). If C is obtuse one gets instead the classical Lagrange-Legendre form F(a) + F(b) = F(c). Gauss's version is the symmetric 'angle-sum' form. It needs mu < 1 for a real statement.

(2) Equilateral test case a = b = c, A = B = C. The law of cosines gives cos a = cos^2 a + sin^2 a cos A. Rearranged: cos A - cos a + (1 - cos A) cos^2 a = 0, so cos A = cos a/(1 + cos a). Hence sin^2 A = (1 + 2 cos a)/(1 + cos a)^2. Using 1 + 2 cos a = sin(3a/2)/sin(a/2), Gauss gets sin A = 2 sin(a/2)^(3/2) sin(3a/2)^(1/2) / sin^2 a. Then 1/mu = sin a/sin A = sin^3 a / (2 sin(a/2)^(3/2) sin(3a/2)^(1/2)). In this last line he writes sin(3a/2) without the exponent 1/2. For an equilateral triangle the theorem says 3F(a) = 2K, i.e. a is the 'trisection' amplitude for this modulus.

(3) Numerical work, with logs to base 10 using the -10 convention. The argument x is the side in units of a right angle, so a = 90 x degrees; x = 0.2 gives a = 18 degrees. For x = 0.2, 0.3, 0.35 the columns contain:
- 3 log sin a: 8.4699, 8.9711, 9.1543;
- (3/2) log sin(a/2): 8.7915, 9.0523, 9.1505;
- their difference: 9.6784, 9.9188, 0.0038;
- log sin(3a/2): 9.6570, 9.8125, 9.8659;
- Q(x) = log[ sin^3 a / (sin(a/2)^(3/2) sin(3a/2)) ]: 0.0214, 0.1063, 0.1379.
The target is log sqrt2 = 0.1505150. Seven-figure refinements follow:
- x = 0.372 (a = 33.48 degrees): 9.2249812 - 9.1891546 = 0.0358266, minus 9.8856478, gives Q = 0.1501788;
- x = 0.3726: Q = 0.1505032 (one block reads 0.1505572);
- x = 0.3728: Q = 0.1506113.
Interpolation gives x ~ 0.3726-0.3727, a ~ 33.54 degrees. Fractions 118/540 = 59/270 appear to be interpolation factors. Q = log sqrt2 is equivalent to his written expression sin^3 a/(2 sin(a/2)^(3/2) sin(3a/2)) = 1/sqrt2, which looks like an attempt at a lemniscatic or other special modulus. The extra digit strings (3681853, 684092, 4594364, 7297182, 4603426 ...) are further log entries for log sin(a/2), its half, and (3/2) log sin(a/2) at the trial points.

RIGHT PAGE. (4) Inversion. Gauss defines u = integral of dphi/sqrt(A + B cos phi) and its inverse phi = F(u). A struck attempt reads F(phi+psi) = ...; he also writes F(u - w) = m. Then (F'(u))^2 = A + B cos phi.

(5) Lagrange-type derivation of the addition theorem. Put dt = dphi/sqrt(A + B cos phi), phi = F(t), psi = F(t + a). Then phi'^2 = A + B cos phi and psi'^2 = A + B cos psi. Differentiating gives 2 phi'' + B sin phi = 0 and 2 psi'' + B sin psi = 0, the pendulum equation. Subtracting the first integrals gives (phi' + psi')(phi' - psi') = B(cos phi - cos psi).

Claim: (phi' + psi')/sin((phi - psi)/2) = C and (phi' - psi')/sin((phi + psi)/2) = C' are constants. To verify the first, d/dt of the quotient vanishes iff (phi'' + psi'') sin((phi - psi)/2) = (1/2)(phi' + psi')(phi' - psi') cos((phi - psi)/2). Substituting the ODE and the difference identity, this becomes -(1/2)B(sin phi + sin psi) sin((phi - psi)/2) = (1/2)B(cos phi - cos psi) cos((phi - psi)/2). This is a trig identity: both sides equal -B sin((phi + psi)/2) sin((phi - psi)/2) cos((phi - psi)/2).

Constancy of C' follows (my step) because C C' = (phi'^2 - psi'^2)/[sin((phi - psi)/2) sin((phi + psi)/2)] = -2B. The algebraic addition theorem is therefore sqrt(A + B cos phi) + sqrt(A + B cos psi) = C(a) sin((phi - psi)/2). This is the integral of dphi/sqrt(A + B cos phi) + ... with psi = F(t + a), where C is fixed by phi = 0, psi = F(a).

The upside-down fragments at the bottom (fractions with powers of e, a sin/cos expression) are illegible and unrelated.
